<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The story of L’Eroica, Tuscany’s white gravel roads and the Atala Sanremo in the collection."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/stories/leroica/toscana-illustration.webp"/><title>When old bicycles found new roads · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/leroica-gamle-cykler-nye-veje/"/><main><article class="eroica-page">
<header class="eroica-hero"><p class="section-label">L’Eroica · Tuscany · 1997</p><h1>When old bicycles<br/>found <em>new roads</em></h1><p>An old racing bicycle need not stand still to be history. It can still be ridden.</p></header>
<figure class="eroica-bike"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/leroica/atala-sanremo.webp`} alt="Red and yellow Atala Sanremo racing bicycle from the mid-1980s"/><figcaption>Atala Sanremo · mid-1980s · Campagnolo Nuovo Record.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="eroica-opening"><p class="section-label">A small beginning</p><div><p>In 1997, just 92 cyclists lined up in the small Tuscan town of Gaiole in Chianti. They did something that, at the time, seemed almost backwards.</p></div></section>
<section class="eroica-story"><div class="eroica-copy"><p>While the bicycle industry pursued aluminium, carbon fibre, clipless pedals and ever more advanced gearing, they brought out old steel bicycles, wool jerseys and toe clips and rode onto Tuscany’s dusty white gravel roads.</p><p>It was the beginning of L’Eroica.</p><p>Its founder, Giancarlo Brocci, wanted to recover something from the cycling he felt was disappearing: the effort, the fellowship and the landscape. The event also helped draw attention to preserving the old <em>strade bianche</em> — the white gravel roads that have since become a symbol of the area.</p></div><aside><strong>92</strong><span>riders at the first<br/>L’Eroica in October 1997</span></aside></section>
<figure class="eroica-landscape"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/leroica/toscana-illustration.webp`} alt="Illustration of riders on classic racing bicycles on a white gravel road in Tuscany" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A contemporary illustration of the spirit and landscape of L’Eroica — not a historical photograph.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="eroica-rules"><div><p class="section-label">Bici Eroica</p><h2>The old equipment is taken seriously</h2><p>As a rule, a classic Bici Eroica must have been built no later than 1987. The year marks the period when toe clips and straps began to give way in earnest to modern clipless pedals.</p></div><dl>{rules.map(([l,v])=><div><dt>{l}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>)}</dl></section>
<section class="eroica-detail-grid"><figure><img src={`${base}/images/stories/leroica/gearlevers.webp`} alt="Campagnolo gear levers mounted on the Atala’s down tube" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The gear levers sit on the down tube, as required by the L’Eroica regulations.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${base}/images/stories/leroica/front-brake.webp`} alt="Gipiemme front brake and visible brake cable on the Atala Sanremo" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Visible brake cable and a classic side-pull brake.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-portrait"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/leroica/front.webp`} alt="Front wheel, handlebars and head tube of the Atala Sanremo" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Steel frame, classic handlebars and external cables.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-portrait"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/leroica/frame.webp`} alt="Frame and Campagnolo Nuovo Record gears of the Atala Sanremo" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Atala Sanremo with Campagnolo Nuovo Record.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="collection-ready"><p class="section-label">The collection is nearly ready for the start</p><blockquote>GIOS 1975.<br/>Rossin 1978.<br/>Faggin 1987.</blockquote><p>Several bicycles in the collection could almost be ridden straight to the start — with the 1987 Faggin right on the boundary.</p></section>
<section class="strade-section"><div><p class="section-label">From nostalgia to professional racing</p><h2>The white roads return</h2></div><div><p>In 2007, a professional race was held on the same Tuscan gravel roads under the name Monte Paschi Eroica. Alexandr Kolobnev won the first edition.</p><p>The race later changed its name and is now known as Strade Bianche. A small nostalgic event with 92 people on old racing bicycles thus helped bring the white gravel roads all the way back into modern professional cycling.</p></div></section>
<section class="eroica-closing"><blockquote>History is not only something you look at.<br/><em>It is also something you ride.</em></blockquote></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><p>The history and equipment rules have been checked against L’Eroica’s official history and current regulations. The connection to the professional race is also described by the UCI.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.eroica.cc/en/ethos-en/">L’Eroica: history and classic ethos</a></li><li><a href="https://www.eroica.cc/en/regulations-leroica/">L’Eroica: regulations for historical bicycles</a></li><li><a href="https://fr.uci.org/article/les-strade-bianche-sont-l-%C3%A9quivalent-printanier-d-un-grand-%C3%A9v%C3%A9nement-m%C3%A9di%C3%A9val-d-%C3%A9t%C3%A9/3AOvPJUpp172Z9fmS5BPW3">UCI: the history of Strade Bianche</a></li></ul></div></section>
